-- ============================================================================
-- 4. THE THREE LAYERS
-- ============================================================================

/*
Snowflake is a HYBRID of two classic designs:
  - Shared-disk     : all compute reads one central copy of data (like SAN)
  - Shared-nothing  : each compute node processes its own slice in parallel (MPP)
  Snowflake = central storage (shared-disk) + independent MPP clusters
  (shared-nothing) that cache data locally.

  +-------------------------------------------------------------------+
  |  CLOUD SERVICES LAYER  ("the brain")                              |
  |  Authentication | Access control | Metadata | Query optimizer     |
  |  Transaction mgmt | Infrastructure mgmt | RESULT CACHE            |
  +-------------------------------------------------------------------+
           |                     |                       |
  +----------------+   +----------------+   +----------------+
  | WAREHOUSE A    |   | WAREHOUSE B    |   | WAREHOUSE C    |  COMPUTE LAYER
  | (load_wh)      |   | (transform_wh) |   | (bi_wh)        |  ("the muscle")
  | local SSD cache|   | local SSD cache|   | local SSD cache|
  +----------------+   +----------------+   +----------------+
           |                     |                       |
  +-------------------------------------------------------------------+
  |  STORAGE LAYER  (Amazon S3, managed by Snowflake)                 |
  |  Micro-partitions: columnar, compressed, immutable, encrypted     |
  +-------------------------------------------------------------------+

LAYER 1 - STORAGE
  - Data is stored in Snowflake-managed S3 in a proprietary columnar format.
  - You cannot see these files directly; you only access them via SQL.
  - Billed per TB per month (average compressed bytes stored), including
    Time Travel and Fail-safe bytes.

LAYER 2 - COMPUTE (Virtual Warehouses)
  - A warehouse = a cluster of compute nodes (CPU, memory, local SSD).
  - Warehouses NEVER compete with each other: a heavy ELT job on
    transform_wh cannot slow down dashboards on bi_wh.
  - All warehouses see the same data (single source of truth).
  - Billed in credits per second while running (60-second minimum each
    time a warehouse starts/resumes).

LAYER 3 - CLOUD SERVICES
  - Always on, shared across the account, managed by Snowflake.
  - Parses and optimizes queries, stores metadata, enforces security,
    coordinates transactions.
  - Billed ONLY if daily cloud services usage exceeds 10% of daily
    warehouse compute credits (the first 10% is free).
  - Things that drive cloud-services cost: very high volumes of tiny
    queries, heavy SHOW/DESCRIBE/INFORMATION_SCHEMA calls, complex query
    compilation, many COPY commands listing huge numbers of files.

INTERVIEW ANSWER - "Why is separation of storage and compute important?"
  1. Scale independently: store petabytes cheaply; pay for compute only
     when queries run.
  2. Workload isolation: separate warehouses for loading, transformation
     and BI - no resource contention.
  3. Elasticity: resize or add clusters in seconds without moving data.
  4. Cost control: suspended warehouses cost nothing; storage is cheap.
*/

-- ============================================================================
-- 5. MICRO-PARTITIONS & PRUNING [***]
-- ============================================================================

/*
WHAT IS A MICRO-PARTITION?
  - Snowflake automatically splits every table into micro-partitions as
    data is loaded - no PARTITION BY clause needed.
  - Each micro-partition holds 50-500 MB of UNCOMPRESSED data
    (much smaller after compression).
  - Data inside is stored COLUMN by COLUMN and compressed per column.
  - Micro-partitions are IMMUTABLE: an UPDATE or DELETE writes NEW
    micro-partitions and marks the old ones as removed (the old ones
    are kept for Time Travel/Fail-safe).

METADATA STORED FOR EVERY MICRO-PARTITION (in cloud services):
  - Range of values (MIN / MAX) for each column
  - Number of distinct values, NULL count
  - Row count, byte size

PRUNING - how Snowflake skips data:
  Example: orders table with 10,000 micro-partitions, loaded day by day.

    SELECT SUM(amount) FROM orders WHERE order_date = '2026-10-01';

  The optimizer checks MIN/MAX of order_date per micro-partition and
  only reads partitions whose range contains 2026-10-01. If data was
  loaded in date order, maybe 30 of 10,000 partitions are scanned.
  ->  99.7% of data is never read.

  In the Query Profile: "Partitions scanned: 30 / Partitions total: 10000"

WHAT BREAKS PRUNING:
  - Data loaded in random order for the filter column (wide MIN/MAX ranges
    overlap in every partition) -> fix with clustering (section 36)
  - Functions on the filter column:  WHERE TO_CHAR(order_date,'YYYY') = '2026'
    -> rewrite as: WHERE order_date BETWEEN '2026-01-01' AND '2026-12-31'
  - Filtering on a column with values spread across all partitions
  - Joining on a column without a filter (no static pruning; Snowflake can
    still do dynamic/runtime pruning on joins)

OVERLAP & DEPTH (clustering vocabulary):
  - Overlap: how many micro-partitions share a range of values for a column
  - Clustering depth: average number of overlapping partitions at a point.
    Lower depth = better pruning. A perfectly clustered table has depth ~1.

IMPORTANT CONSEQUENCES OF IMMUTABILITY (interview favourites):
  - Frequent single-row UPDATE/DELETE is expensive: each one rewrites whole
    micro-partitions. Batch DML instead (MERGE many rows at once).
  - Heavy churn increases Time Travel/Fail-safe storage (old versions kept).
  - Zero-copy cloning is possible BECAUSE partitions never change in place.
*/

/* Metadata-only queries: answered from cloud services, no warehouse needed */
SELECT COUNT(*), MIN(order_ts), MAX(order_ts) FROM sales_db.curated.orders;

/* Check how well a table is clustered on a column */
SELECT SYSTEM$CLUSTERING_INFORMATION('sales_db.curated.orders', '(order_date)');

-- ============================================================================
-- 6. QUERY LIFECYCLE - WHAT HAPPENS WHEN YOU RUN A QUERY [***]
-- ============================================================================

/*
  SELECT region, SUM(amount) FROM orders WHERE order_date >= '2026-10-01'
  GROUP BY region;

  STEP 1  (Cloud Services) Authenticate user, check role privileges.
  STEP 2  (Cloud Services) Check RESULT CACHE: same query text + same data
          + same role privileges within 24h?  -> return instantly, no
          warehouse needed, no compute cost.
  STEP 3  (Cloud Services) Parse + optimize: pick join order, apply
          pruning using micro-partition metadata -> list of partitions
          to scan.
  STEP 4  (Compute) Resume warehouse if suspended (AUTO_RESUME). If the
          warehouse is busy, the query QUEUES.
  STEP 5  (Compute) Each node reads its assigned partitions - first from
          LOCAL SSD CACHE, otherwise from remote storage (S3).
  STEP 6  (Compute) Execute: scan -> filter -> join -> aggregate. If memory
          is not enough, data SPILLS to local disk, then to remote storage
          (slow!).
  STEP 7  (Cloud Services) Store result in result cache, return to client.

  INTERVIEW TIP: Being able to walk through this sequence shows you
  understand where time and money are spent: steps 1-3 are cloud services,
  steps 4-6 consume warehouse credits.
*/

-- ============================================================================
-- 7. CACHING LAYERS [***]
-- ============================================================================

/*
  +----------------+-------------------+-------------------+------------------+
  |                | RESULT CACHE      | WAREHOUSE (LOCAL  | METADATA CACHE   |
  |                |                   | DISK) CACHE       |                  |
  +----------------+-------------------+-------------------+------------------+
  | Lives in       | Cloud services    | SSD of warehouse  | Cloud services   |
  | Stores         | Final query result| Raw micro-        | Partition stats, |
  |                |                   | partition data    | row counts       |
  | Lifetime       | 24h, extended each| Until warehouse   | Always           |
  |                | reuse, max 31 days| suspends/resizes  |                  |
  | Shared across  | All users/WHs     | Only that         | Everyone         |
  |                | (same role privs) | warehouse         |                  |
  | Needs WH?      | NO                | YES               | NO               |
  | Cost           | Free              | Saves S3 reads    | Free             |
  +----------------+-------------------+-------------------+------------------+

RESULT CACHE IS REUSED ONLY IF:
  - Query text is identical (case and whitespace sensitive in practice)
  - Underlying data has not changed
  - No non-deterministic functions (CURRENT_TIMESTAMP(), RANDOM(), UUID_STRING())
  - Role has the required privileges
  - USE_CACHED_RESULT = TRUE (default)

AUTO_SUSPEND TRADE-OFF (cache vs. cost):
  - Suspending a warehouse drops its SSD cache.
  - BI warehouses with repeated queries: slightly longer AUTO_SUSPEND
    (e.g. 5-10 min) keeps the cache warm.
  - ETL warehouses that run a job and stop: short AUTO_SUSPEND (60s).
*/

/* Demonstrate the cache layers */
ALTER SESSION SET USE_CACHED_RESULT = FALSE;   -- turn off result cache for testing
SELECT region, SUM(amount) FROM sales_db.curated.orders GROUP BY region;  -- cold: reads S3
SELECT region, SUM(amount) FROM sales_db.curated.orders GROUP BY region;  -- warm: SSD cache
ALTER SESSION SET USE_CACHED_RESULT = TRUE;
SELECT region, SUM(amount) FROM sales_db.curated.orders GROUP BY region;  -- result cache

/* Reuse the result of the previous query without re-running it */
SELECT * FROM TABLE(RESULT_SCAN(LAST_QUERY_ID())) WHERE "REGION" = 'US';

-- ============================================================================
-- 8. VIRTUAL WAREHOUSES [***]
-- ============================================================================

/*
SIZES & CREDITS (Standard Gen1 warehouse, per hour while running)
  +--------+---------+     Each size up DOUBLES compute and DOUBLES cost.
  | Size   | Credits |     If a query scales linearly, a 2x bigger
  +--------+---------+     warehouse finishes in half the time for the
  | XS     |    1    |     SAME total credits - so bigger can be "free"
  | S      |    2    |     and faster for large queries.
  | M      |    4    |
  | L      |    8    |     Billing: per second, 60-second minimum each
  | XL     |   16    |     time it starts/resumes or is resized up.
  | 2XL    |   32    |
  | 3XL    |   64    |
  | 4XL    |  128    |
  | 5XL    |  256    |
  | 6XL    |  512    |
  +--------+---------+

WAREHOUSE TYPES
  - Standard (Gen1)        : default
  - Standard Gen2          : newer hardware + engine optimizations; faster
                             for DML/MERGE-heavy ELT, at a higher per-hour
                             credit rate - benchmark before switching
  - Snowpark-optimized     : ~16x memory per node; for ML training, large
                             Python UDFs/procedures, memory-heavy workloads

SCALE UP vs SCALE OUT  [*** classic question]
  SCALE UP   (bigger size: M -> L)
    - Fixes: slow COMPLEX queries, spilling to disk, big joins/sorts
    - Does NOT fix: many users waiting in a queue
  SCALE OUT  (multi-cluster: 1 -> N clusters of the same size, Enterprise)
    - Fixes: CONCURRENCY - many queries queuing at the same time
    - Does NOT make a single query faster

MULTI-CLUSTER MODES
  - Maximized : MIN_CLUSTER_COUNT = MAX_CLUSTER_COUNT (always N clusters)
  - Auto-scale: MIN < MAX; clusters added/removed based on load

SCALING POLICY (auto-scale mode)
  - STANDARD (default): adds a cluster as soon as queries queue;
                        favours performance
  - ECONOMY           : adds a cluster only if there is enough load to keep
                        it busy ~6 minutes; favours cost, tolerates queuing

WORKLOAD ISOLATION PATTERN (recommended)
  load_wh       XS-S,   auto-suspend 60s   - COPY / Snowpipe-adjacent work
  transform_wh  M-L,    auto-suspend 60s   - dbt / tasks / MERGE
  bi_wh         S-M,    multi-cluster 1-4, auto-suspend 300-600s - dashboards
  adhoc_wh      S,      statement timeout  - analysts / data science
*/

CREATE OR REPLACE WAREHOUSE transform_wh WITH
    WAREHOUSE_SIZE      = 'MEDIUM'
    AUTO_SUSPEND        = 60          -- seconds idle before suspending
    AUTO_RESUME         = TRUE        -- start automatically when a query arrives
    INITIALLY_SUSPENDED = TRUE        -- do not start (and bill) at creation
    STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_IN_SECONDS = 3600   -- kill runaway queries after 1h
    COMMENT = 'ELT transformations';

CREATE OR REPLACE WAREHOUSE bi_wh WITH
    WAREHOUSE_SIZE    = 'SMALL'
    MIN_CLUSTER_COUNT = 1
    MAX_CLUSTER_COUNT = 4             -- scale out for concurrency
    SCALING_POLICY    = 'STANDARD'
    AUTO_SUSPEND      = 300
    AUTO_RESUME       = TRUE;

CREATE OR REPLACE WAREHOUSE load_wh WITH
    WAREHOUSE_SIZE = 'XSMALL' AUTO_SUSPEND = 60 AUTO_RESUME = TRUE
    INITIALLY_SUSPENDED = TRUE;

CREATE OR REPLACE WAREHOUSE adhoc_wh WITH
    WAREHOUSE_SIZE = 'SMALL' AUTO_SUSPEND = 120 AUTO_RESUME = TRUE
    INITIALLY_SUSPENDED = TRUE
    STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_IN_SECONDS = 1800;    -- analysts can't run 2-day queries

/* Snowpark-optimized warehouse for memory-heavy Python work */
CREATE OR REPLACE WAREHOUSE snowpark_wh WITH
    WAREHOUSE_SIZE = 'MEDIUM'
    WAREHOUSE_TYPE = 'SNOWPARK-OPTIMIZED'
    AUTO_SUSPEND = 60;

/* Resize on the fly (running queries finish on old size, new queries
   use the new size) */
ALTER WAREHOUSE transform_wh SET WAREHOUSE_SIZE = 'LARGE';
ALTER WAREHOUSE transform_wh SET WAREHOUSE_SIZE = 'MEDIUM';

/* Common pattern: size up only for a heavy step inside a job */
-- ALTER WAREHOUSE transform_wh SET WAREHOUSE_SIZE = 'XLARGE';
-- CALL heavy_backfill();
-- ALTER WAREHOUSE transform_wh SET WAREHOUSE_SIZE = 'MEDIUM';

SHOW WAREHOUSES;

-- ============================================================================
-- 9. CONCURRENCY, QUEUING & TIMEOUTS
-- ============================================================================

/*
  - MAX_CONCURRENCY_LEVEL (default 8): target number of queries a single
    cluster runs in parallel. Extra queries QUEUE (or trigger a new cluster
    in multi-cluster mode).
  - STATEMENT_QUEUED_TIMEOUT_IN_SECONDS (default 0 = wait forever):
    cancel queries that wait in the queue too long.
  - STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_IN_SECONDS (default 172800 = 2 days): cancel queries
    that RUN too long. Setting this is a key COST GUARDRAIL.
  - Parameters can be set at ACCOUNT, WAREHOUSE, USER or SESSION level;
    the most specific level wins (lowest of warehouse and session applies
    for timeouts).

  How to diagnose:
    - High QUEUED_OVERLOAD_TIME in QUERY_HISTORY -> concurrency problem
      -> scale OUT (multi-cluster) or split workloads.
    - High execution time + spilling       -> scale UP or fix the query.
    - QUEUED_PROVISIONING_TIME              -> warehouse was resuming.
*/

ALTER WAREHOUSE bi_wh SET
    MAX_CONCURRENCY_LEVEL = 8
    STATEMENT_QUEUED_TIMEOUT_IN_SECONDS = 600
    STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_IN_SECONDS = 1800;

/* Who is queuing? */
SELECT warehouse_name,
       COUNT(*)                                 AS queries,
       AVG(queued_overload_time) / 1000         AS avg_queue_sec,
       AVG(execution_time) / 1000               AS avg_exec_sec
FROM SNOWFLAKE.ACCOUNT_USAGE.QUERY_HISTORY
WHERE start_time >= DATEADD(day, -7, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP())
GROUP BY warehouse_name
ORDER BY avg_queue_sec DESC;

