-- ============================================================================
-- 46. ARCHITECTURE QUESTIONS
-- ============================================================================

/*
Q1. Explain Snowflake's architecture.
A.  Three layers: (1) STORAGE - data in Snowflake-managed S3 as immutable,
    compressed, columnar micro-partitions; (2) COMPUTE - independent virtual
    warehouses (MPP clusters) with local SSD cache; (3) CLOUD SERVICES -
    auth, metadata, optimizer, transactions, result cache. It is a hybrid of
    shared-disk (one central copy of data) and shared-nothing (MPP compute).
    Because storage and compute are separate, many warehouses can work on
    the same data at once without contention, and each scales independently.

Q2. What are micro-partitions and how does pruning work?
A.  Automatic 50-500 MB (uncompressed) columnar units. Cloud services keeps
    min/max/distinct/null metadata per column per partition. At compile time
    the optimizer compares the WHERE predicates with that metadata and skips
    partitions that cannot match. Pruning efficiency = partitions scanned /
    total in the Query Profile.

Q3. Why can't I just add an index in Snowflake?
A.  There are no traditional indexes. Instead: natural clustering by load
    order, clustering keys for range filters, Search Optimization Service for
    point lookups, and MVs/DTs for precomputation. (Hybrid tables do support
    indexes for OLTP-style workloads.)

Q4. Explain the three caches. Which one survives a warehouse suspend?
A.  Result cache (cloud services, 24h, survives suspend, needs identical
    query + unchanged data), warehouse SSD cache (lost on suspend/resize),
    metadata cache (always available - COUNT(*), MIN/MAX answered without a
    warehouse). Only result + metadata caches survive suspension.

Q5. Scale up vs scale out?
A.  Scale up = bigger warehouse = faster complex queries, fixes spilling.
    Scale out = multi-cluster = more concurrent queries, fixes queuing.
    A single slow query never gets faster by adding clusters.

Q6. A query is fast in the morning but slow at 9-10 AM. Why?
A.  Most likely concurrency: queries queue when dashboards refresh. Check
    QUEUED_OVERLOAD_TIME. Fix: multi-cluster (auto-scale), separate BI and
    ELT warehouses, move ELT out of business hours. Also check cold cache
    after an overnight suspend.

Q7. What happens to running queries when you resize a warehouse?
A.  Running queries finish on the current resources; new/queued queries use
    the new size. Size-up adds nodes (billed at new rate, 60s minimum for the
    added nodes); size-down removes them after running queries complete.

Q8. How is data protected / recoverable in Snowflake?
A.  Time Travel (0-90 days, user-driven: AT/BEFORE, UNDROP, clone),
    Fail-safe (7 more days, Snowflake support only, permanent tables),
    replication/failover groups to another region, zero-copy clones as
    instant backups. Encryption at rest/in transit always on.

Q9. What is the difference between transient and temporary tables?
A.  Both have no Fail-safe and max 1 day Time Travel. Transient persists
    until dropped and is visible to other sessions; temporary exists only
    for the session that created it.

Q10. How does zero-copy cloning work and when does it start costing money?
A.  The clone references the same micro-partitions (metadata copy). Cost
    starts only when either side modifies data, because modified data goes
    into new micro-partitions owned by that object.

Q11. Why are frequent single-row updates bad in Snowflake?
A.  Micro-partitions are immutable: each update rewrites whole partitions,
    creating new versions and Time Travel/Fail-safe storage, plus table-level
    DML locks serialize concurrent updates. Batch changes into one MERGE.

Q12. How does Snowflake handle concurrency and consistency?
A.  MVCC via immutable micro-partition versions; readers never block
    writers. Statement-level READ COMMITTED isolation. UPDATE/DELETE/MERGE on
    the same table are serialized by locks; INSERT/COPY can run concurrently.

Q13. What is Snowflake's role in a lakehouse / how do you avoid lock-in?
A.  Iceberg tables keep data in open Parquet/Iceberg format in your own S3,
    readable by Spark/Trino/Athena, while Snowflake provides DML, governance
    and performance. External tables query S3 files read-only.

Q14. What are the cloud services charges and how can they become high?
A.  Free up to 10% of daily warehouse credits. They spike with huge numbers
    of tiny queries (e.g. BI tools polling, single-row inserts), heavy
    SHOW/INFORMATION_SCHEMA usage, very complex query compilation, and COPY
    commands scanning stages with millions of files.
*/

