-- 47. COST OPTIMIZATION QUESTIONS
-- ============================================================================

/*
Q1. Your Snowflake bill doubled this month. How do you investigate?
A.  1) METERING_DAILY_HISTORY by service_type: is it warehouses or
       serverless (clustering, pipes, MVs, SOS)?
    2) WAREHOUSE_METERING_HISTORY by warehouse and by day/hour: which
       warehouse and when did it start?
    3) QUERY_ATTRIBUTION_HISTORY: top queries / users / query tags by credits.
    4) Check for changes: new dbt models, a warehouse resized and never sized
       back, AUTO_SUSPEND changed, new multi-cluster, a task schedule
       tightened, DT TARGET_LAG lowered, a new clustering key on a high-churn
       table, a full-refresh job replacing an incremental one.
    5) Fix + add guardrails: resource monitors, budgets, statement timeouts,
       alerts on daily credit spikes.

Q2. How do you choose a warehouse size?
A.  Start small, benchmark the real workload on 2-3 sizes. If runtime halves
    when size doubles, cost is flat and speed improves - go bigger. When the
    speed-up flattens, stop. Look for spilling (too small) and very short
    queries on large warehouses (too big). Use separate warehouses per
    workload with sizes tuned to each.

Q3. What does AUTO_SUSPEND cost you if set too high or too low?
A.  Too high: paying for idle time (e.g. 10 min idle after every 1-min job).
    Too low on BI: frequent cold starts lose the SSD cache and pay the 60s
    minimum each resume. ETL: 60s; BI: 300-600s.

Q4. Serverless task vs warehouse task - which is cheaper?
A.  Serverless: per-second, no idle time, no 60s minimum - cheaper for short
    or infrequent tasks and avoids over-sizing. Warehouse: cheaper for long
    heavy jobs or when many tasks can share an already-running warehouse
    (serverless compute carries a price multiplier).

Q5. How do you reduce storage costs?
A.  Transient for staging/raw, right-size Time Travel retention, avoid daily
    full reloads of permanent tables (each reload creates Time Travel +
    Fail-safe copies), drop unused tables/clones, purge stages, archive cold
    data. Use TABLE_STORAGE_METRICS to find tables where time-travel +
    fail-safe bytes exceed active bytes.

Q6. Clustering key added and costs went up - why?
A.  Automatic clustering is a serverless service. On a table with heavy
    DML churn (or a high-cardinality key), Snowflake keeps reclustering.
    Check AUTOMATIC_CLUSTERING_HISTORY; consider a coarser key
    (TO_DATE(ts) instead of ts), loading data pre-sorted, suspending
    reclustering during backfills, or removing the key.

Q7. How do resource monitors differ from budgets?
A.  Resource monitors track warehouse credits and can SUSPEND warehouses.
    Budgets track spend (including serverless features) against a monthly
    limit and send notifications/projections; they don't suspend compute.

Q8. How do you implement chargeback per team?
A.  Dedicated warehouses per team/domain + object tags (cost_center) on
    warehouses, QUERY_TAG set by each pipeline/dbt, and per-query cost from
    QUERY_ATTRIBUTION_HISTORY aggregated by tag/user/role.

Q9. Dynamic tables are getting expensive. What would you tune?
A.  Raise TARGET_LAG to what the business really needs; use DOWNSTREAM for
    intermediate DTs; make sure refreshes are INCREMENTAL (check
    refresh_mode_reason); give DTs a right-sized dedicated warehouse;
    suspend DTs in non-prod.

Q10. Snowpipe costs are high. What would you check?
A.  File count vs volume (PIPE_USAGE_HISTORY): millions of tiny files mean
    per-file overhead dominates. Batch files upstream (Firehose buffering,
    100-250 MB) or switch to Snowpipe Streaming for event streams; for big
    periodic batches a scheduled COPY on a warehouse may be cheaper.
*/

