-- 48. PIPELINE & SCENARIO-BASED QUESTIONS (SENIOR LEVEL) [***]
-- ============================================================================

/*
S1. Design a CDC pipeline from an operational database (e.g. Oracle/
    Postgres on RDS) into Snowflake.
A.  - Extract: CDC tool (AWS DMS, Debezium -> Kafka/MSK, Fivetran) writes
      change records (op type I/U/D, commit timestamp, PK) to S3 or Kafka.
    - Ingest: Snowpipe (S3) or Snowflake Kafka connector / Snowpipe
      Streaming into an append-only RAW table (VARIANT + metadata).
    - Process: append-only stream on RAW -> task (triggered or every few
      minutes) -> dedupe to the latest change per PK using commit timestamp
      / LSN (QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER) -> MERGE into curated: D = delete (or soft
      delete), I/U = upsert, only if the change is newer than the target.
    - History: SCD2 dimension or keep RAW as the full change log.
    - Gold: dynamic tables for aggregates.
    - Ops: audit log, alerts, reconciliation counts vs source, monitoring
      stream staleness and pipe status.

S2. Snowpipe loaded duplicate records. What could cause it and how do you fix?
A.  Causes: same data arriving in files with DIFFERENT names (producer
    retries), files overwritten/modified (new ETag), a manual COPY with
    FORCE=TRUE or a COPY and a pipe both loading the same files (separate
    load metadata), reloads after the 14-day pipe metadata window, or the
    source itself sending duplicates.
    Fix: dedupe downstream by business key (QUALIFY + MERGE - make curated
    idempotent regardless of raw duplicates); fix the producer to write
    immutable unique file names; never mix pipe and manual COPY on the same
    path without care; clean existing dups with an INSERT OVERWRITE.

S3. Your stream went stale. What happened and how do you recover?
A.  The stream was not consumed within the table's retention (+ the
    automatic extension up to MAX_DATA_EXTENSION_TIME_IN_DAYS, default 14),
    e.g. the task was suspended for weeks. Recover: recreate the stream,
    then reconcile the missed window - reprocess from RAW using load_ts
    ranges, or compare source vs target and MERGE the differences.
    Prevent: alert on STALE_AFTER, keep tasks running, monitor
    SUSPEND_TASK_AFTER_NUM_FAILURES auto-suspension.

S4. A task runs every 5 minutes but each run now takes 8 minutes.
A.  With ALLOW_OVERLAPPING_EXECUTION = FALSE the next runs are skipped, so
    latency grows. Investigate the Query Profile of the slow step (pruning,
    spilling, MERGE scanning the whole target). Options: prune the MERGE
    target (ON clause on date), dedupe smaller batches, size up the
    warehouse for that step, cluster the target on the join/filter key,
    switch to a dynamic table, or lengthen the schedule if the SLA allows.

S5. How do you guarantee exactly-once processing?
A.  Snowflake gives at-least-once at the edges; you make it effectively
    exactly-once by: file-level dedup (load metadata), consuming streams in
    a transaction (offset moves only on commit), deduping each batch, and
    MERGE on a business key with "only apply if newer" conditions. Re-runs
    then produce the same result (idempotent).

S6. How do you handle late-arriving data?
A.  Base incremental logic on load time (stream / load_ts), not event time;
    MERGE with updated_at comparison so older changes don't overwrite newer
    ones; for aggregates re-compute a lookback window or use DTs; for SCD2,
    insert the late version into the correct position and fix neighbouring
    effective dates.

S7. Load 2 years / 20 TB of historical files that already sit in S3.
A.  Scheduled COPY (not Snowpipe) on a temporarily larger warehouse, split
    by month prefix in a loop, ON_ERROR=SKIP_FILE + COPY_HISTORY review,
    load in date order for natural clustering, suspend downstream tasks/DTs
    and reclustering while loading, load into a new table and SWAP after
    validation, then size the warehouse back down. Check file sizes first -
    if there are millions of tiny files, compact them (e.g. in Glue/EMR)
    before loading.

S8. The source added/removed/renamed columns. How does your pipeline cope?
A.  RAW in VARIANT absorbs additions. For typed loads use
    MATCH_BY_COLUMN_NAME + ENABLE_SCHEMA_EVOLUTION. Renames/removals: the
    curated model maps fields explicitly, so missing fields become NULL and
    DQ checks (null counts) alert; a contract/schema registry in front of
    the pipeline catches breaking changes. Version the transformation in Git
    and deploy through CI/CD.

S9. A MERGE fails with "Duplicate row detected during DML action".
A.  More than one source row matches the same target row
    (ERROR_ON_NONDETERMINISTIC_MERGE). Deduplicate the source by the merge
    key with QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() ... = 1 ordered by the change timestamp.

S10. Someone ran DELETE without a WHERE on a production table an hour ago.
A.  Find the query_id in QUERY_HISTORY; CREATE TABLE x_restore CLONE x
    BEFORE (STATEMENT => '<query_id>'); validate; ALTER TABLE x SWAP WITH
    x_restore (or INSERT the missing rows back). Then tighten RBAC so
    humans don't have DELETE on prod tables.

S11. How would you build near-real-time (< 1 minute) dashboards?
A.  Kafka -> Snowpipe Streaming (seconds latency) -> dynamic tables with a
    short TARGET_LAG (or a stream+triggered task) -> BI on a multi-cluster
    warehouse. Discuss the trade-off: lower lag = more refreshes = more cost;
    agree the real SLA with the business.

S12. How do you test and deploy pipeline changes safely?
A.  Git + CI/CD (dbt/schemachange/Terraform). Clone PROD into a test
    database (zero-copy), run the new code against it, compare outputs
    (row counts, checksums, dbt tests), then promote. For large table
    changes: build new version -> validate -> SWAP. Suspend tasks/DTs while
    altering objects in a task graph.

S13. A dashboard query was fast last week and is slow now.
A.  Compare Query Profiles: more partitions scanned (table grew, clustering
    degraded, new filter on an unclustered column), spilling (data volume
    grew), queuing (more users), cache misses (warehouse suspended, data
    changes more often), exploding join (duplicates introduced upstream -
    check dimension uniqueness).

S14. How do you monitor pipelines in production?
A.  TASK_HISTORY / COMPLETE_TASK_GRAPHS, COPY_HISTORY, PIPE_STATUS,
    DYNAMIC_TABLE_REFRESH_HISTORY, stream staleness; an audit table with
    run_id, counts and status; ALERT objects + error integrations (SNS/
    email); DQ via data metric functions; freshness checks on load_ts;
    cost dashboards from ACCOUNT_USAGE.

S15. Streams+Tasks or Dynamic Tables - which would you pick and why?
A.  Dynamic tables for declarative join/aggregate layers where a freshness
    lag is acceptable - less code, automatic dependencies, incremental
    refresh. Streams+Tasks when I need procedural control: SCD2, hard
    deletes with custom rules, multi-target routing, calling external
    services, notifications, or exact control over when processing runs.
    Many mature platforms use both: streams/tasks for silver, DTs for gold.

S16. How do you process a stream into two different target tables?
A.  Either two separate streams (one per consumer) or consume once inside an
    explicit transaction (BEGIN; INSERT INTO a ...; INSERT INTO b ...;
    COMMIT;) - both statements see the same stream contents, and the offset
    moves once at commit. A multi-table INSERT ALL also works.

S17. How do you secure PII in a healthcare (PHI) pipeline?
A.  Business Critical edition (HIPAA), PrivateLink, network policies, SSO +
    MFA, key-pair auth for services. RBAC with access/functional roles and
    least privilege; tag PII columns and apply tag-based masking policies;
    row access policies for entitlements; secure views for sharing;
    ACCESS_HISTORY for auditing; mask or tokenize in non-prod clones.

S18. How do you size and schedule warehouses for a nightly batch window?
A.  Group tasks by dependency into a DAG; run independent branches in
    parallel on one appropriately sized warehouse (or multi-cluster to avoid
    queuing); size up only for the heavy step; AUTO_SUSPEND 60 so it stops
    the moment the DAG finishes; resource monitor on the warehouse;
    measure with TASK_HISTORY and QUERY_ATTRIBUTION_HISTORY per run.
*/

