-- 50. WHY SNOWFLAKE? SNOWFLAKE vs AMAZON REDSHIFT
-- ============================================================================

/*
THE PROBLEM SNOWFLAKE WAS BUILT TO SOLVE
  Traditional MPP warehouses (Teradata, Netezza, early Redshift) tied storage
  and compute together on the same nodes. That caused:
    - Need more storage? Buy more compute nodes too (and vice versa).
    - ETL and BI fight for the same cluster -> slow dashboards during loads.
    - Resizing = redistributing data across nodes -> downtime / hours.
    - DBAs spend time on distribution keys, sort keys, VACUUM, ANALYZE,
      workload queues and index-like tuning.
    - Semi-structured data (JSON) needs flattening before loading.
    - Sharing data = exporting files or copying tables to another cluster.
  Snowflake's answer: fully separate storage from compute, make compute
  elastic and isolated, and remove almost all tuning/administration.

HOW REDSHIFT WORKS (for comparison)
  - Cluster of leader + compute nodes. Classic DC2 nodes store data locally;
    RA3 nodes use Redshift Managed Storage (S3-backed) which partially
    separates storage from compute.
  - Redshift Serverless: capacity in RPUs, auto-scaling, pay per use.
  - Tables are tuned with DISTSTYLE / DISTKEY (how rows spread across nodes)
    and SORTKEY (pruning via zone maps). Automatic table optimization and
    auto-vacuum now reduce, but don't fully remove, this work.
  - Workload Management (WLM) queues + Concurrency Scaling handle concurrency.
  - Redshift Spectrum queries S3 files; SUPER type for semi-structured data.

SIDE-BY-SIDE
  +-------------------------+----------------------------+------------------------------+
  | Area                    | Snowflake                  | Amazon Redshift              |
  +-------------------------+----------------------------+------------------------------+
  | Storage vs compute      | Fully separated from day 1 | Coupled on DC2; separated on |
  |                         |                            | RA3 / Serverless             |
  | Workload isolation      | Many independent warehouses| One cluster shared via WLM   |
  |                         | on the same data           | queues; data sharing between |
  |                         |                            | RA3 clusters/serverless      |
  | Scaling                 | Resize in seconds; multi-  | Elastic resize (minutes),    |
  |                         | cluster auto scale-out     | concurrency scaling, or      |
  |                         |                            | Serverless RPU scaling       |
  | Idle cost               | Auto-suspend to $0 compute | Provisioned clusters bill    |
  |                         |                            | 24x7 unless paused;          |
  |                         |                            | Serverless bills on use      |
  | Tuning                  | No dist/sort keys, no      | DISTKEY/SORTKEY, VACUUM,     |
  |                         | VACUUM; optional clustering| ANALYZE (largely automated   |
  |                         | keys                       | now but still a design task) |
  | Semi-structured         | VARIANT, FLATTEN, automatic| SUPER + PartiQL; works, less |
  |                         | columnarization of JSON    | mature for heavy JSON use    |
  | Time Travel / cloning   | Time Travel up to 90 days, | Snapshots & restore; no      |
  |                         | UNDROP, zero-copy clones   | instant zero-copy clones     |
  | Data sharing            | Live cross-account, cross- | Data sharing within AWS      |
  |                         | region sharing, Marketplace| (RA3/Serverless), AWS Data   |
  |                         |                            | Exchange                     |
  | Multi-cloud             | AWS, Azure, GCP + cross-   | AWS only                     |
  |                         | cloud replication          |                              |
  | Native CDC/ELT          | Streams, Tasks, Dynamic    | Materialized views (auto     |
  |                         | Tables, Snowpipe           | refresh), Zero-ETL from      |
  |                         |                            | Aurora/RDS, streaming ingest |
  |                         |                            | from Kinesis/MSK             |
  | AWS integration         | Good (S3, IAM, PrivateLink,| Deepest (IAM, Glue catalog,  |
  |                         | SNS/SQS)                   | Lake Formation, SageMaker,   |
  |                         |                            | QuickSight, Zero-ETL)        |
  | Pricing model           | Credits per second +       | Node-hours (reserved         |
  |                         | storage per TB             | instances discounts) or RPU- |
  |                         |                            | hours + managed storage      |
  +-------------------------+----------------------------+------------------------------+

WHY TEAMS MOVE FROM REDSHIFT TO SNOWFLAKE (typical interview answer)
  1. Concurrency: BI users stop waiting behind ETL - each workload gets its
     own warehouse on the same data.
  2. Less operations: no distribution/sort key design, no vacuum windows,
     resizes in seconds instead of planned maintenance.
  3. Elastic cost: pay per second only while running; dev/test warehouses
     cost nothing when idle.
  4. Semi-structured data: land JSON directly in VARIANT, query immediately.
  5. Data protection & DevOps: Time Travel, UNDROP, zero-copy clones for
     instant dev/QA environments.
  6. Data sharing / collaboration with partners without copying data.
  7. Multi-cloud / multi-region strategy.

WHEN REDSHIFT IS STILL A GOOD CHOICE (be balanced in interviews)
  - Organisation is all-in on AWS and wants native integration
    (Zero-ETL from Aurora, Lake Formation, SageMaker, QuickSight).
  - Steady 24x7 predictable workload where reserved-instance pricing is
    cheaper than on-demand credits.
  - Existing Redshift expertise, tooling and data already there - migration
    cost/risk outweighs the benefits.
